+ 20. Rxf2 Qc5 { White will struggle to hold on. Next we can aim for Ng4. Best continuation for both: } 21. Ne7+ Kf8 22. Qe1 Rxb2 23. Nxc8 Ng4 24. Ne4 Qd4) 15... Bxd3 16. Qa1 e4 17. Ne5 Bxf1 18. Kxf1 Rd2) 10... Rd8 { b4 is actually not the best move, so there's no need to stop it with a5. } 11. b4 Bb6 12. Qc2 { 42% } (12. Nd2 { 13% } 12... Bg4 { When white has Ne4 playable, we go Bg4 instead of Bf5. } 13. h3 Rxd3 14. Qc2 Rxg3 15. hxg4 Nd4 16. Qd1 Nxg4 17. Nf3 { The only move that doesn't lead to mate for white! Other examples: } 17... Rd8 18. fxg3 Nxe2+ 19. Kh1 Nxg3#) 12... Bf5 13. Bb2 Rac8 14. Rac1 Nd4 15. Qd1 e4 { Most common move is a blunder: } 16. Nxd4 Bxd4 { bkm:PRIO_1 Best continuation for white: } { Second most common move is a blunder: }) (4. e3 { 6% } 4... e5 5. a3 { White is probably going for b5 -> Bb2, we avoid our regular Bc5 plan. } 5... Nf6 6. Nc3 Be7 { With b5 played or ready to go, we skip Bc5 and settle with Be7. } 7. b4 O-O 8. Bb2 Bf5 9. Bc4 Rc8 { If white goes d3, we go e4. If white goes Ba2, we go a4. Our default plan is to connect the rooks with Qd7 and bring the other rooks to the center with Rfd8. }) 4... e5 5. g3 { 36% } (5. Nf3 { 19% } 5... Nf6 6. d3 Bc5 { Normally, we'd stop Bg5 with h6 - but in this case Bg5 would be a bad move due to Qb6. Also, if white goes for e3, we don't have time to waste on h6. } 7. g3 { 40% } (7. e3 { 27% This is the most difficult setup to face. } 7... O-O 8. Be2 Qe7 { With the pawn on e3, playing h6 is not necessary. } 9. O-O Rd8 10. Qc2 Bb6 { Stepping away from Ne4 prematurely, so that if Bf5 and Ne4 happens, we can respond with Rac8 instead of backing away with the bishop. } 11. a3 Bf5 12. Ne4 { 36% } (12. b4 { 21% } 12... Rac8 13. Bb2 Nd4 14. exd4 exd4) 12... Rac8 13. Qb1 { Only move that keeps any advantage for white. Qa4 leads to some disco potential: } 13... Nd5 14. Bd2 Bg6 { Making room for f5. } 15. Rc1 f5 16. Nc3 f4 { White's best continuation is to trade on d5: }) (7. Bg5 { 20% } 7... Qb6 8. e3 Qxb2 9. Qc1 Ba3 { Qb4 is best even without check } 10. Ne2 Qb4+ 11. Qd2 e4 12. Qxb4 Nxb4 13. dxe4 Nc2+ 14. Kd2 Nxa1 { bkm:PRIO_1 }) 7... e4 { bkm:MEMORY_MARKER White has only pushed one central pawn and haven't played Bg2 yet - We go e4! } 8. dxe4 { 75% } (8. Ng5 { 11% } 8... exd3 9. Qxd3 Qxd3 10. exd3 Nd4 11. Kd2 h6 12. Nge4 Nxe4+ 13. Nxe4 Bb4+ 14. Nc3 Bg4 15. Bg2 O-O-O) 8... Qxd1+ 9. Nxd1 Nb4 10. Rb1 Nc2+ 11. Kd2 Nb4 { Most common move is a big blunder } 12. a3 (12. Nc3 Bf5 13. e3 { If white actually grabs the bishop: } 13... Nxe4+ 14. Nxe4 Bxe4) 12... Nxe4+ 13. Ke1 Nc2#) (5. d3 Nf6 { bkm:New_5d3 No need to stop Bg5 with h6, since it's a pretty bad move. } 6. Bg5 { 6. g3 and 6.Nf3 should transpose into previous variations where white didn't start with 5.d3. } 6... Qb6 7. Bxf6 (7. Rb1 Be6 8. Bxf6 gxf6 { We're not scared of this structure. It's actually better for black. Next we can castle long and go Bc5. } 9. g3 O-O-O { With Bc5 coming next. For example: } 10. Bg2 Bc5 11. Na4 Bxf2+ 12. Kf1 Qe3 13. Nh3 Bxh3 14. Bxh3+ Kb8 { Or if white blocks: } 15. Bg2) 7... Qxb2 8. Rc1 gxf6 9. g3 Bb4 10. Qd2 Qa3 11. Bg2 Nd4 12. e3 Nb5 13. Nge2 Nxc3 14. Nxc3 O-O 15. O-O Be6 { Get a rook to the c-file and we win the knight }) (5. e4 { 12% } 5... Nf6 6. Nf3 Bc5 7. Bb5 { 46% } (7. Bc4 { 18% } 7... O-O 8. O-O Bg4 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Nd4 11. Qd1 b5 12. Nxb5 Nxb5 13. Bxb5 Nxe4 { Tactical continuation:  Bc6 is a blunder! } 14. Qe2 Ng3 15. Qxe5 Bd6 { and we capture the rook next } 16. Bc6) (7. Be2 { 17% } 7... O-O 8. O-O Qe7 9. d3 h6 10. a3 Rd8 11. b4 Bb6 12. Qc2 Bg4) 7... O-O 8. O-O Nd4 9. Nxe5 { 43% } (9. Nxd4 { 21% } 9... exd4 10. Na4 Bd6 { Keep an eye out for greek gift! It doesn't work if white plays Be2, d3 or f4 next. }) (9. Be2 { Not as common 17% as taking on e5 or d4 - but this is white's best move. } 9... Nxf3+ { Not worth it to take the bishop. The bishop is more awkward on f3 than the knight.  @@StartCAL@@%cal Gd4f3@@EndCAL@@@@StartCSL@@%csl Gf3@@EndCSL@@ } 10. Bxf3 Qd6 { If white goes after our queen: } 11. d3 Rd8 12. Qe2 b6 { Important! We can't grab on d3 yet due to Rd1 skewer. } 13. Rd1 Ba6 14. Bg5 h6 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 16. Nd5 Qd6 17. Rac1 Bb7 18. a3 a5 { The position is equal, even though we're down a pawn. We have the bishop pair, and white has the backward pawn on d3. Next can go Rac8. a4 might also be good. }) 9... Qc7 10. Nf3 { 59% } (10. Nd3 { 41% } 10... Bg4 11. Qa4 Bb6 12. e5 a6 { We play this against most of white moves that doesn't threaten the queen } 13. Qc4 { If white takes on f6: } 13... axb5 14. Qxc7 Bxc7 15. exf6 Rfd8 { We don't care about taking on f6 - go after white's knights! The position is -2.5 even though we're 'down 2 pawns'. }) 10... Ng4 11. g3 Qb6 { Don't forget that we have three attackers on f2: } 12. Nxd4 Bxd4 13. Qe2 f5 { Go for the king! }) (5. e3 { 11% } 5... Nf6 6. Bb5 Bd6 { Bc5 isn't as good here, white would play Nf3 putting pressure on e5. bkm:TODO:_CONTINUE_HERE } 7. Nge2 { 28% } (7. Bxc6+ { 28% } 7... bxc6 8. Nge2 { 30% } (8. Nf3 { 30% } 8... O-O 9. O-O e4 10. Nd4 Bxh2+ 11. Kxh2 Ng4+ 12. Kg3 h5 13. Nxe4 h4+ 14. Kf3 Ne5+ 15. Ke2 Ba6+) 8... O-O 9. d4 e4 10. O-O Re8 11. Ng3 h5 12. Nxh5 Bxh2+ 13. Kxh2 Ng4+ 14. Kg3 Qg5 15. Nf4 Nxe3+) (7. Nf3 { 26% } 7... O-O 8. O-O e4 9. Ng5 Bxh2+ 10. Kxh2 Ng4+ 11. Kg1 Qxg5 12. f3 exf3 13. Qxf3 Be6 { Candidate moves next are h5, Qg6, Rd8. }) 7... O-O 8. O-O e4 9. d3 exd3 10. Bxd3 Qe7 11. h3 Rd8) 5... Nf6 6. Bg2 Bc5 7. e3 { 35% } (7. d3 { 31% } 7... O-O 8. Nf3 { If white plays Bg5 before Nf3 we have Bxf7 tactics: } 8... h6 { After white has played Nf3, Bg5 is a threat. Now Bxf7 tactics don't work. So it's best to prevent Bg5 as soon as Nf3 is played. } 9. O-O Qe7 10. Bd2 Rd8 11. Rc1 Bf5 12. Qa4 Rac8 13. a3 Bb6 14. b4 Nd4 15. Nxd4 { Another natural move was Be3, which allows a nice tactic: } 15... exd4 16. Ne4 Nxe4 { We'll win the back the pawn, at least:  Even if they play correctly, we'll end up with an annoying pawn on d3. } 17. Bg5) (7. Nf3 { 27% } 7... O-O 8. O-O h6 9. d3 { 71% } (9. e3 { 12% } 9... e4 { bkm:MEMORY_MARKER If we control h5 and white hasn't played d3 to stop e4 - we should play e4. } 10. Ne1 Re8 11. d4 Bb4 12. a3 Bxc3 { It looks wrong to give white more central pawns, but they'll be weak and easy targets. After Bd6, white wont play c4 easily. } 13. bxc3 Be6 14. Nc2 Na5 { White has really weak light squares, stockfish evaluates it to -1.7. We'll probably win the pawn on c3, and we could try get a knight to d3. } 15. Rb1 Rc8 16. Re1 { We can likely grab the pawn soon, but there might be better moves - Bg4, a6, b6 etc. }) 9... Qe7 10. a3 { 61% } (10. Nd2 { 11% } 10... Bg4 { When white goes for Ne4 like this, we go Bg4 instead of Bf5. Allowing us to play Nd4 later with tempo on e2. } 11. Nde4 Nxe4 12. Nxe4 Nd4 13. Re1 Bb4 14. Bd2 Bxd2 15. Qxd2 f5 16. Nc3 f4) 10... Rd8 11. b4 Bb6 12. Bb2 e4 13. Ne1 { Not as common, but possible: } 13... Bf5 14. Rc1 Rac8 15. Na4 Bd4 16. Bxd4 Nxd4 17. Rxc8 Rxc8 18. dxe4 Rd8 { bkm:PRIO_1 Only move that works! White can't take the bishop due to the disco Ne2+.  If the queen simply hides: }) 7... Nb4 8. d4 exd4 9. exd4 Qxd4 10. Qxd4 Bxd4 11. Kd2 Bf5 12. a3 Nc2 13. Ra2 O-O-O { bkm:PRIO_1000 } *
